  "title": "Eldgjá eruption",
  "summary": "The Eldgjá fissure eruption in southern Iceland began around 939 CE and produced about 20 km3 of lava and tephra; the Icelandic Met Office calls it the largest effusive basaltic eruption known in the Katla volcanic system and the most recent of its kind. After it, glacial floods from Katla began running to the south-east plains instead of west of Mýrdalsjökull.",
